Of course! Here's the code for a simple button in the most common contexts.

### 1. Basic HTML Button
This is the fundamental button element you can use anywhere.
```html
<button>Click Me</button>
```

### 2. Styled Button (HTML + CSS)
To make it look nicer, add some CSS.
```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
<style>
  .my-button {
    background-color: #4CAF50; /* Green */
    border: none;
    color: white;
    padding: 12px 24px;
    text-align: center;
    text-decoration: none;
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 16px;
    margin: 4px 2px;
    cursor: pointer;
    border-radius: 8px;
  }

  .my-button:hover {
    background-color: #45a049;
  }
</style>
</head>
<body>

<button class="my-button">Click Me</button>

</body>
</html>
```

### 3. Button in React (JSX)
If you're using a JavaScript framework like React:
```jsx
function MyButton() {
  const handleClick = () => {
    alert('Button was clicked!');
  };

  return (
    <button onClick={handleClick} className="my-button">
      Click Me
    </button>
  );
}
```

### 4. Button in Vue.js
Here's an example using Vue's template syntax:
```vue
<template>
  <button class="my-button" @click="handleClick">
    Click Me
  </button>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  methods: {
    handleClick() {
      alert('Button was clicked!');
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

### Key things to know:
- **`<button>`** is the standard, accessible element for buttons.
- You can change its look using **CSS** (`background-color`, `border-radius`, etc.).
- To make it **do something**, you use an event handler like **`onclick`** (HTML), **`onClick`** (React), or **`@click`** (Vue).

If you need a button for a **specific framework** (like Angular, Svelte) or a **specific style** (like a primary, danger, or icon button), just let me know! I can give you the exact code you need.
